Simple linear laceration from slip with hand tool to forearm
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Linear forearm dermal defect, Hand tool slip mechanism, Intact distal neurovascular status

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-023
Reason for Visit: Acute, gaping forearm laceration following a tool slip, requiring urgent hemostasis, decontamination, and primary closure.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a deep-appearing laceration on the forearm caused by a sharp utility tool. Urgent clinical evaluation was necessary to assess for underlying muscle fascia violation, clear neurovascular compromise, and perform primary suturing within the optimal timeframe to prevent secondary infection prior to the next daytime clinic.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My utility knife slipped and cut my forearm deeply; it gapes open when I move my wrist.
History of Present Illness: Injury occurred following a sudden slip or misdirection while applying force with a sharp manual tool. The patient reports a linear, full-thickness dermal wound located on the volar aspect of the forearm. Initial moderate bleeding slowed and ceased quickly with continuous direct manual pressure. The patient experiences sharp, localized pain confined strictly to the exact site of the dermal disruption. Patient emphatically denies any loss of feeling in the hand or weakness in the fingers.
Functional Status: Fully independent; restricting forearm supination to avoid pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Engaging in DIY repairs; patient advised to clear the workspace safely.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 132/82 | HR: 84 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Alert, applying moderate pressure to the forearm, visually inspecting the wound edges.
Physical Exam: Forearm examination reveals a 4cm linear full-thickness dermal wound. The wound edges are clean and sharply defined, characteristic of a blade edge. Subcutaneous fat is visible, but the underlying muscle fascia remains fully intact and unbreached upon direct wound exploration. Distal neurovascular status is completely intact: normal distal pulses are palpated at the radial and ulnar arteries, and sensation is completely intact across the median, ulnar, and radial nerve distributions of the hand. Active wrist and finger range of motion is intact in both flexion and extension axes without triggering deep pain.
Environmental Observations: Utility knife safely retracted and placed aside.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Simple linear laceration to forearm - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Muscle belly/fascial laceration - Ruled out due to: Direct visual exploration confirming the underlying fascial sheath is completely intact without muscular herniation.

Arterial laceration (Radial/Ulnar) - Ruled out due to: Non-pulsatile bleeding easily controlled with pressure, and strong palpable distal pulses.

Tendon/Nerve laceration - Ruled out due to: Normal sensory examination and fully preserved active range of motion of the wrist and digits.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

A laceration to the volar forearm from a sharp tool inherently threatens underlying superficial nerves, tendons, and vasculature. Rigorous bedside neurological and physical assessment, including direct wound exploration, confidently ruled out deep structural involvement. Urgent after-hours assessment and intervention were clinically mandated to thoroughly decontaminate the wound of environmental debris and execute primary sutured closure, rapidly halting bleeding and mitigating the high risk of infection that would accompany a delay until daytime services.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered local anesthesia (Lidocaine 1% with Epinephrine). Irrigated vigorously with 500mL sterile normal saline. Wound closed via simple interrupted non-absorbable sutures to handle the dynamic tension of the forearm. Tetanus prophylaxis updated.
Positive Behavioral Change: Wear a simple tubular compression bandage over the dressing for 48 hours. This applies mild, continuous counter-pressure, preventing blood from pooling under the skin flap, which directly prevents painful hematoma formation and reduces tension on the stitches.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Tubular compression bandage.
Follow-up: Routine GP or practice nurse review in 10-14 days for suture removal.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department if the fingers suddenly become pale, cold, or numb, if bleeding restarts profusely through the bandage, or if red streaks track up the arm.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Deep tissue swelling can sometimes evolve over 24 hours; monitor the limb for any sudden onset of severe, unremitting tightness.
Clinical Course: Mild stinging and bruising around the wound are normal for 3-5 days. Sutures must remain dry and undisturbed to ensure maximum tensile strength prior to removal.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical attention if your forearm becomes rock-hard and agonizingly painful to touch, or if thick yellow pus begins to drain from the stitches.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Closing the wound with stitches prevents environmental bacteria from entering the deep fat layer and ensures the scar heals as a thin line rather than a thick, wide patch.
Actionable Steps: Keep the wound clean and completely dry for 48 hours. Avoid heavy lifting with that arm. Do not forcefully stretch the forearm skin.

